# Google Form Autofiller

Capture values from any support tool (Farzi, Uniware, Shiprocket, ClickPost, GoQuick, etc.)
with a right-click, then fill matching Google Form fields with one click — no tab-switching,
no retyping, no forms resetting your progress.

## Two kinds of fields

- **Dynamic** — changes every call (Order ID, AWB Number, Phone Number...). You capture it
  fresh each time via right-click, then hit Fill.
- **Fixed** — never changes (your name, team, department, a standard note...). You type the
  value once in *Manage Fields*, and from then on it **fills itself automatically** on every
  Google Form — on first load, after a refresh, and after "Submit another response" — no
  click needed.

## How it works

1. **Define fields once** — ships with three dynamic fields (Order ID, AWB Number, Phone
   Number). Add more, and mark any of them **Fixed** with a value, under *Manage fields*
   (opens as a side panel next to the field list).
2. **Capture dynamic values on any page** — select the value's text (or click into an input
   that has it), right-click, choose **Capture as ▸ Order ID**. Saved instantly, with a toast
   confirming it. Fixed fields don't appear in this menu — you only set their value once.
3. **Fixed fields fill themselves** — the moment a Google Form (or a new blank one after
   submitting) renders, any fixed field with a matching label is filled automatically, with
   no click. Dynamic fields still need the **Fill Google Form** button, since their value is
   different every time.
4. **Fill Google Form** button fills both fixed and dynamic fields at once, fuzzy-matching
   your field labels/aliases against the form's question labels (so "Order ID", "Order No.",
   "Order Number" all match), and only touches free-text fields it's confident about —
   dispatching real input/change events so Google Forms' own validation passes.
5. Captured dynamic values persist across form resets, so submitting one form and opening
   the next doesn't lose anything. "Clear captured" wipes dynamic values only — fixed
   values are kept until you edit or remove them yourself.

You can also edit or manually paste a value straight into the popup's field row (useful the
first time, or if a page can't be scripted).

## Install (unpacked, for testing/personal use)

1. Unzip this folder somewhere permanent (don't delete after installing — Chrome loads
   the extension from these files).
2. Go to `chrome://extensions`.
3. Turn on **Developer mode** (top right).
4. Click **Load unpacked** and select this folder.
5. Pin the extension (puzzle-piece icon → pin) for one-click access.

> If this is a work laptop, IT may need to allow Developer Mode / unpacked extensions —
> some MDM policies restrict this.

## Notes on accuracy

- Auto-fill only touches **short-answer and paragraph (free text)** questions — it
  intentionally never clicks radio buttons, checkboxes, or dropdowns, since a wrong
  auto-click there is worse than skipping a field.
- Matching uses normalized text + Levenshtein similarity (threshold ~72% similarity), so
  minor label differences ("AWB" vs "AWB Number" vs "Tracking ID" if added as an alias)
  still match, but unrelated fields won't.
- Every filled field gets a brief blue highlight so you can visually confirm what changed
  before submitting — always give the form a glance before hitting submit.
- Add aliases (comma-separated) per field in *Manage fields* to teach it your specific
  forms' exact wording for even tighter matching.

## Files

- `manifest.json` — extension config (Manifest V3)
- `background.js` — right-click menu + storage
- `content-capture.js` — runs on all pages, remembers what you right-clicked
- `content-autofill.js` — runs on Google Forms, does the matching + filling
- `lib/fuzzy.js` — label normalization + Levenshtein similarity scoring
- `popup.html/.css/.js` — the "box" UI showing captured values and the Fill button

